1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,690
Most people will tell you that
this is a wedding portrait,

2
00:00:03,560 --> 00:00:05,850
and that the bride is pregnant.

3
00:00:06,350 --> 00:00:11,460
It is a lovely story, and guidebooks
have repeated it for generations.

4
00:00:11,960 --> 00:00:17,790
But look at what is actually in front of
us, because almost none of it holds up.

5
00:00:18,290 --> 00:00:22,480
Jan van Eyck finished the
Arnolfini Portrait in 1434,

6
00:00:22,590 --> 00:00:25,920
and it has puzzled art
historians ever since.

7
00:00:26,420 --> 00:00:32,150
It is small, an oil painting on an oak
panel just eighty-two centimetres tall and

8
00:00:32,020 --> 00:00:37,450
sixty wide, yet it crams in more evidence
than most rooms full of paintings.

9
00:00:37,950 --> 00:00:42,600
Notice the mirror on the back wall, and
the Latin words painted just above it,

10
00:00:42,470 --> 00:00:46,820
which say that Jan van
Eyck was here, in 1434.

11
00:00:47,320 --> 00:00:51,190
A painter does not usually
sign his name like a witness.

12
00:00:51,690 --> 00:00:56,480
So who was he watching, and
what exactly was he witnessing?

13
00:00:56,980 --> 00:01:02,450
Over the next few minutes we will look at
this picture in three layers: what we can

14
00:01:02,320 --> 00:01:07,210
see with our own eyes, what the documents
and historians actually tell us,

15
00:01:07,240 --> 00:01:14,010
and how all of that might be read, bearing in mind
that no painting has ever held only one meaning.

16
00:01:19,960 --> 00:01:23,870
To understand the couple, we first
have to understand their city.

17
00:01:24,370 --> 00:01:29,880
In the early fifteenth century, booming
trade produced a new rung on the social

18
00:01:29,750 --> 00:01:32,880
ladder, a class of rich
and powerful merchants,

19
00:01:32,750 --> 00:01:36,760
and nowhere were they more at
home than Bruges, in Flanders,

20
00:01:37,030 --> 00:01:39,600
which is today part of northern Belgium.

21
00:01:40,100 --> 00:01:44,510
Bruges was an international
crossroads, where wool, spices,

22
00:01:44,900 --> 00:01:47,450
silk and furs arrived
from every direction,

23
00:01:47,320 --> 00:01:52,690
and where ambitious people came from across
the known world to make their fortunes.

24
00:01:53,190 --> 00:01:59,300
One of those families came from Lucca,
in Tuscany, and was called Arnolfini.

25
00:01:59,800 --> 00:02:03,690
As the merchants got richer, their
hunger for prestige grew with them,

26
00:02:03,560 --> 00:02:07,170
and the surest sign that you
had arrived was to be painted,

27
00:02:07,400 --> 00:02:12,270
which in the 1430s meant being
painted by one particular man.

28
00:02:12,770 --> 00:02:17,840
Jan van Eyck was a trailblazer
of Northern Renaissance painting.

29
00:02:18,340 --> 00:02:23,870
In 1425 he took a post as painter to
Philip the Good, the Duke of Burgundy,

30
00:02:24,060 --> 00:02:30,350
and by 1431 he had settled in Bruges, where
in 1432 he finished the enormous Ghent

31
00:02:30,220 --> 00:02:33,630
Altarpiece, yet he kept
accepting private clients,

32
00:02:33,800 --> 00:02:36,690
and portraits remained
central to his career.

33
00:02:37,190 --> 00:02:41,660
So a portrait by Van Eyck
was far more than a likeness.

34
00:02:42,160 --> 00:02:47,030
In that decade, no purchase
announced your success more loudly.

35
00:02:50,480 --> 00:02:55,470
Who bought this one? A surviving
inventory entry points to a merchant named

36
00:02:55,340 --> 00:03:02,670
Giovanni di Nicolao Arnolfini, who had
taken Costanza Trenta as his wife in 1426.

37
00:03:03,170 --> 00:03:06,500
His fortune came from silks
and other luxury cloth,

38
00:03:06,750 --> 00:03:09,680
along with tapestries
and precious objects.

39
00:03:10,180 --> 00:03:15,810
The marriage produced no recorded
children, and Costanza was dead by 1433,

40
00:03:15,960 --> 00:03:19,210
a full year before Van
Eyck finished this panel.

41
00:03:19,710 --> 00:03:22,500
We should be honest about the limits here.

42
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:27,750
Other scholars name a different
Arnolfini, or doubt that the woman can be

43
00:03:27,620 --> 00:03:32,530
identified at all, and Van Eyck painted
a separate portrait of a man from this

44
00:03:32,400 --> 00:03:35,910
family, now in Berlin, a few years later.

45
00:03:36,410 --> 00:03:42,880
The names are probable, not proven. What
is certain is how unusual the picture is.

46
00:03:43,380 --> 00:03:49,290
It shows two people who were neither
royalty nor nobility, in their own home,

47
00:03:49,420 --> 00:03:54,930
at a time when almost every such picture
was made for a court or a church.

48
00:03:55,430 --> 00:03:58,900
For many years the picture was
read as a marriage portrait,

49
00:03:59,170 --> 00:04:03,820
a kind of visual contract with the
figures in the mirror acting as witnesses.

50
00:04:04,320 --> 00:04:07,590
But without any evidence
that Giovanni remarried,

51
00:04:07,820 --> 00:04:11,930
a memorial to Costanza starts to
look like the stronger reading,

52
00:04:11,800 --> 00:04:15,390
and I find that the more
convincing of the two.

53
00:04:15,890 --> 00:04:21,100
Look down at the dog at her feet. In
art, dogs usually stand for loyalty,

54
00:04:20,970 --> 00:04:23,840
and that may be all this one means.

55
00:04:24,340 --> 00:04:29,430
Yet on late medieval tombs, sculptors
often placed a dog by a woman's feet,

56
00:04:29,300 --> 00:04:33,050
because people believed it would
go with her into the afterlife.

57
00:04:33,550 --> 00:04:36,820
Portraits of the dead
were not unusual either.

58
00:04:37,320 --> 00:04:43,390
Titian, Holbein and Bronzino each painted
some, relying on death masks, drawings,

59
00:04:43,620 --> 00:04:48,310
descriptions or older likenesses to
capture a face that was no longer there.

60
00:04:48,810 --> 00:04:54,100
Whatever else it does, this double
portrait pins a prosperous couple firmly to

61
00:04:53,970 --> 00:04:55,640
their place in society.

62
00:04:59,090 --> 00:05:04,760
Clothes shape identity, then as now,
and here every thread is a statement.

63
00:05:05,260 --> 00:05:09,410
What strikes you first about
Costanza is that green dress,

64
00:05:09,280 --> 00:05:11,850
bunched up in her hand at the front.

65
00:05:12,350 --> 00:05:17,740
People took that for pregnancy, but artists
of the time used exactly this pose to

66
00:05:17,610 --> 00:05:21,180
signal fertility, which
was prized in a wife.

67
00:05:21,680 --> 00:05:26,710
The colour alone is an announcement.
To get wool that deep and even,

68
00:05:26,580 --> 00:05:30,870
a team of dyers had to labour
for weeks, combining plants,

69
00:05:31,120 --> 00:05:35,670
herbs and imported spices in a
long series of chemical steps.

70
00:05:36,170 --> 00:05:41,480
Now look at the lining. Inside, the
gown is faced with the pale belly fur of

71
00:05:41,350 --> 00:05:45,900
squirrels, called miniver, and where an
ordinary woman's gown took around six

72
00:05:45,770 --> 00:05:50,240
hundred pelts, one of this size
took as many as two thousand.

73
00:05:50,740 --> 00:05:55,910
Notice how carefully she handles
blue. Because blue was so costly,

74
00:05:56,060 --> 00:06:01,390
it belonged to the Virgin Mary, so Costanza
shows just a flash of blue underdress

75
00:06:01,260 --> 00:06:05,370
beneath the fur, which signals
money without boasting about it.

76
00:06:05,870 --> 00:06:08,620
Giovanni is dressed with the same care.

77
00:06:09,120 --> 00:06:14,290
His clothes are plum and black, dyed by
an even pricier process than the green,

78
00:06:14,560 --> 00:06:19,050
his hat is straw, woven in tiny plaits,
the newest fashion out of Italy,

79
00:06:19,120 --> 00:06:23,990
and his velvet tabard, made from silk,
nods to the weavers of his home city,

80
00:06:24,490 --> 00:06:29,080
and is lined with the pelts of roughly
a hundred pine martens from Russia.

81
00:06:29,580 --> 00:06:35,010
His boots are such supple leather that
they show the outline of each foot.

82
00:06:35,510 --> 00:06:40,460
And in the lower left corner, you can
spot the pointed overshoes called pattens,

83
00:06:40,330 --> 00:06:43,140
caked with mud from a recent walk.

84
00:06:43,640 --> 00:06:48,790
An abandoned pair of shoes was often
read as a symbol of a sacred moment.

85
00:06:49,290 --> 00:06:53,800
But they also remind us that this
is a real room, on a real day.

86
00:06:57,250 --> 00:07:01,200
The room will strike us
as strange, and it is.

87
00:07:01,700 --> 00:07:05,390
Everything in it was picked
to signal comfort and rank,

88
00:07:05,260 --> 00:07:08,650
without seeming too grand for a merchant.

89
00:07:09,150 --> 00:07:13,580
Look at the bed behind the
couple. A bed suggests a bedroom,

90
00:07:13,790 --> 00:07:16,960
yet bedrooms were private,
almost holy places,

91
00:07:17,170 --> 00:07:20,580
and the mirror shows two
visitors being welcomed in.

92
00:07:21,080 --> 00:07:26,590
So this is a reception room, where a costly
bed stood on show and was never slept

93
00:07:26,460 --> 00:07:28,790
in, purely to impress.

94
00:07:29,290 --> 00:07:34,180
Further back is a bench called a
settle, carved with grotesque creatures,

95
00:07:34,050 --> 00:07:36,680
with a red velvet cushion on top.

96
00:07:37,180 --> 00:07:41,790
Comfort was rare in those days,
so a cushion counted as a luxury,

97
00:07:41,660 --> 00:07:45,810
and the carved monsters, thought
to frighten off evil spirits,

98
00:07:45,940 --> 00:07:48,930
sit right above the couple's joined hands.

99
00:07:49,430 --> 00:07:54,540
Beside it stands a tall-backed chair,
topped by a woman riding a dragon.

100
00:07:55,040 --> 00:07:58,870
That could be Saint Martha,
patron saint of housewives,

101
00:07:59,180 --> 00:08:02,270
and the brush hanging
nearby is her emblem.

102
00:08:02,770 --> 00:08:06,780
Now look at the floor. Van
Eyck loved painting rugs,

103
00:08:06,650 --> 00:08:10,640
because they let him show off his
command of texture and pattern,

104
00:08:10,510 --> 00:08:14,120
and this oriental carpet
is beautifully done.

105
00:08:14,620 --> 00:08:18,130
Such rugs often appear in
pictures of the Virgin Mary,

106
00:08:18,000 --> 00:08:21,290
so it may hint at Costanza's piety.

107
00:08:21,790 --> 00:08:26,500
Set it beside the carpet in the altarpiece
he painted for Canon van der Paele,

108
00:08:26,750 --> 00:08:29,020
finished the very same year.

109
00:08:29,520 --> 00:08:35,350
The same patient looking runs through both,
down to the way light settles in the pile.

110
00:08:35,850 --> 00:08:39,600
Beside the mirror hangs a
string of amber prayer beads,

111
00:08:39,790 --> 00:08:44,900
the sort of present a groom gave his bride,
and each bead holds its own highlight,

112
00:08:45,010 --> 00:08:50,480
with a green silk thread, probably laid in
with a one-hair brush, running through them.

113
00:08:50,980 --> 00:08:54,570
Finally, let your eye drift to the window.

114
00:08:55,070 --> 00:09:02,460
Glass was expensive, and the oranges on the sill
were imports that only the very rich could buy.

115
00:09:02,960 --> 00:09:07,630
Study the single orange, and you will find
that it throws a shadow and is mirrored

116
00:09:07,500 --> 00:09:14,070
by the windowpane, a perfect still life painted
before anyone thought of still life as a subject.

117
00:09:17,520 --> 00:09:20,950
And now to the most intriguing
object in the picture.

118
00:09:21,450 --> 00:09:27,920
The mirror pulls the whole room together, adding
depth and life to an otherwise quiet scene.

119
00:09:28,420 --> 00:09:32,110
In the fifteenth century glass
mirrors were high technology,

120
00:09:32,580 --> 00:09:35,490
and they were usually
convex, like this one.

121
00:09:35,990 --> 00:09:39,720
But a mirror so wide could
not actually be made then,

122
00:09:39,890 --> 00:09:42,460
so Van Eyck simply enlarged it.

123
00:09:42,960 --> 00:09:47,590
Look closely at the reflection. There
is the couple, seen from the back,

124
00:09:47,460 --> 00:09:52,830
and two more people stepping through a doorway
that must lie on our side of the picture.

125
00:09:53,330 --> 00:09:58,080
One of them, dressed in blue, is
sometimes said to be Van Eyck,

126
00:09:58,210 --> 00:10:02,200
though nobody can prove it, and
the other could stand for us.

127
00:10:02,700 --> 00:10:06,150
Mirrors were also used
in art to suggest purity,

128
00:10:06,260 --> 00:10:11,110
since a clean surface shows the truth,
so this one may be there to underline the

129
00:10:10,980 --> 00:10:12,730
sanctity of the union.

130
00:10:13,230 --> 00:10:17,260
Now notice the frame. Ten tiny roundels,

131
00:10:17,450 --> 00:10:19,820
picturing the suffering
and rising of Christ,

132
00:10:20,050 --> 00:10:23,200
are the picture's only
explicit religious references,

133
00:10:23,450 --> 00:10:27,100
and even at that size
the detail is remarkable.

134
00:10:27,600 --> 00:10:33,370
Their positions matter. Every episode
that comes after the death of Christ is

135
00:10:33,240 --> 00:10:39,330
placed beside Costanza: he is lifted from
the cross, laid in the tomb, rises again,

136
00:10:39,520 --> 00:10:43,150
and descends to free the
souls waiting in limbo.

137
00:10:43,650 --> 00:10:47,360
And one more detail, from
the chandelier above.

138
00:10:47,860 --> 00:10:52,550
Up in the chandelier, one candle is
lit and another has been snuffed out,

139
00:10:52,760 --> 00:10:55,570
the dead one hanging above Costanza.

140
00:10:56,070 --> 00:11:00,300
Some read the flame as the eye
of God, or as a wedding custom,

141
00:11:00,490 --> 00:11:05,740
but if you accept the memorial theory,
it contrasts the living with the dead.

142
00:11:09,190 --> 00:11:13,020
Saying that a painting has one
single meaning would be reckless,

143
00:11:13,210 --> 00:11:17,860
but a handful of clues do lean
towards the memorial interpretation,

144
00:11:18,230 --> 00:11:23,600
starting with the dog by her skirt, then
the small scenes on her half of the frame,

145
00:11:23,470 --> 00:11:26,780
and the chance that he is
wearing mourning clothes,

146
00:11:27,280 --> 00:11:31,230
though dark, costly cloth
was also simply fashionable.

147
00:11:31,730 --> 00:11:36,000
Yet the strongest hint is the
way Giovanni takes her hand.

148
00:11:36,500 --> 00:11:40,070
Look at the joined hands, and
notice how light the grip is,

149
00:11:40,060 --> 00:11:43,570
as if she might at any
moment slip out of his grasp.

150
00:11:44,070 --> 00:11:48,420
Then again, the same gesture
has been read as a wedding vow.

151
00:11:48,920 --> 00:11:52,310
The painting can carry
both readings at once,

152
00:11:52,440 --> 00:11:55,950
and perhaps that is why
it still keeps us looking.

153
00:11:56,450 --> 00:12:02,500
The real marvel is how Van Eyck handles
space, depth, colour and texture,

154
00:12:02,570 --> 00:12:08,160
so that we feel we could step through the
flat wooden panel into a mirrored world.

155
00:12:08,660 --> 00:12:13,610
His contemporaries called him an alchemist,
and although he did not invent oil

156
00:12:13,480 --> 00:12:18,210
painting, as early writers
believed, he certainly improved it.

157
00:12:18,710 --> 00:12:22,800
He knew that some pigments
turn almost transparent in oil,

158
00:12:22,990 --> 00:12:27,020
so he built the picture from many
thin layers, which gives it a glow,

159
00:12:26,890 --> 00:12:31,100
and a surface so smooth
that it looks untouched.

160
00:12:31,600 --> 00:12:35,850
Technical studies show that in places
the paint went on quite freely,

161
00:12:36,060 --> 00:12:39,430
and there is even a thumbprint
close to the dog's shadow.

162
00:12:39,930 --> 00:12:47,120
His reach extended far past the Low Countries,
with patrons in England, France and Portugal.

163
00:12:47,620 --> 00:12:53,430
He inspired artists from Albrecht Durer,
whose self-portrait of 1500 shares that

164
00:12:53,300 --> 00:12:58,310
direct gaze, to Velazquez, whose Las
Meninas has its own mirror at the back,

165
00:12:58,180 --> 00:13:01,130
and on to the Pre-Raphaelites.

166
00:13:01,780 --> 00:13:05,010
So let us return to the
question we started with.

167
00:13:05,510 --> 00:13:08,860
Is this a wedding portrait
of a pregnant bride?

168
00:13:09,360 --> 00:13:11,230
Almost certainly not.

169
00:13:11,730 --> 00:13:17,000
It may record a marriage, it may be a
memorial, and perhaps it is a little of both,

170
00:13:17,230 --> 00:13:22,640
but if we follow the clues the painter
left us, the dog, the roundels, the candle,

171
00:13:22,870 --> 00:13:27,840
the dark clothes and the grip of a hand,
it begins to look like something deeper,

172
00:13:28,340 --> 00:13:31,610
a portrait of a love that outlasts death.

173
00:13:32,110 --> 00:13:36,180
And the man who was there to witness
it signed his name above the mirror,

174
00:13:36,050 --> 00:13:37,740
so that we would know.

